course: dictionary
lesson: 13851
title: Old-fashioned words in stories
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to old stories and cosy books. Joggle is one of them. It means to shake or move with small, quick jerks, or to bounce gently, like a car on a bumpy road. «The old cart joggled along the bumpy lane.» The clues are bumpy lane and the slow, rocking ride, so joggled must mean bounced or shook gently.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The old cart bounced gently along the bumpy lane» still makes sense, so your meaning fits. If it did not make sense, you would need to try another meaning.
explain: Joggle can also be a noun. «Mum gave the wobbly table a joggle, and her tea slopped over.» Here a joggle is a small shake or jolt. Everyday talk often uses jiggle or jog instead, and maths and science do not use joggle at all.
fact: Joggle means to shake or move something with small, quick jerks.
fact: «The jam jar joggled on the shelf as the bus went over the bridge.» Joggled means shook gently.
fact: A joggle can be a noun too: «She gave the box a little joggle» means a small shake or jolt.
fact: Joggle is an old-fashioned, cosy word, so you will meet it more in old stories than in everyday talk.
fact: In everyday talk, people more often say jiggle or jog.
exercise: «The old cart joggled along the bumpy lane.» What does joggled mean? | Stopped suddenly and quietly | Flew high into the air | *Shook gently with small bumps | Painted a bright colour
exercise: «She gave the box a little joggle to see what was inside.» In this sentence, what is a joggle? | *A small shake | A kind of hat | A loud noise | A large, heavy box
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to joggle, and is more common in everyday talk? | Paint | Castle | *Jiggle | Sleep
test: «The caravan joggled slowly down the country lane.» What does joggled suggest? | Flew over the hedges | Sang loudly to the birds | Sank into the mud and stopped | *Bounced gently as it rolled
test: Which sentence uses joggle as a noun, meaning a small shake? | The cart will joggle down the lane. | The kettle joggled on the stove. | We joggled to school together. | *Mum gave the table one quick joggle.
exam: A child says the cart joggles because the lane is bumpy. What clue in the sentence shows this? | The horse is brown | It is Tuesday | The cart is red | *The lane is bumpy
